## PILOT — Workflows

Feature and technical work moves through **PILOT**, a lightweight multi-agent process: each piece of work is a
workflow of short, focused agent steps (personas lead, validate or review) with humans at the gates, instead of
one big context carrying the whole history. PILOT's own skills/agents/docs are copied in from
[`Jonas-Eve/pilot`](https://github.com/Jonas-Eve/pilot), not written here; run `/pilot-update` to pull in its updates.

| Workflow | Skill | Produces |
|---|---|---|
| Need | `/pilot-need` | A wiki entry of observable criteria, then a story (and an epic if large), validated by a human at two gates |
| Plan | `/pilot-plan` | Local ADRs and tickets ready to build: the story as is, or split into tasks only when the code checks say so |
| Build | `/pilot-build` | Tests first, then code, checks, review of the local diff, and one PR merged once its CI passes |
| Accept | `/pilot-accept` | The Recette on the deployed main, by a human or an AI, and correction tickets for what failed |
| Report | `/pilot-report` | A qualified report of an abnormal behavior, sent to the right workflow |
| Express | `/pilot-express` | A simplified path (hotfix, core feature) with provisional documentation, for accounts allowed it |

`python3 .pilot/pilot.py workflow list` and `workflow show <id>` give each workflow's steps, actors and gates;
[.pilot/pilot-process.md](.pilot/pilot-process.md) holds the vocabulary and labels, and
[.pilot/pilot-link-runner.md](.pilot/pilot-link-runner.md) how a workflow runs. A GitHub label state machine
tracks where each ticket is; `needs-human` marks a run waiting for a person's answer.

Admin workflows sit beside them: `/pilot-functional-frame` (what the product does in shared terms), `/pilot-technical-frame` (how the code is built),
`/pilot-design-system`, `/pilot-proto` (prototype), `/pilot-persona`, `/pilot-user-flow`. `/pilot-wiki` and `/pilot-architecture` answer a question from those frames;
`/pilot-dashboard` is the project's cockpit. `/pilot-help` lists everything and explains any command.
